00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.070 align:start position:0%
 
hello everyone this is amir from  audiosounds review i have a professional  amplifier that i reviewed yesterday here  is the ashley in e8250  um it's a 8 channel amplifier and costs  fair bit for a pro amplifier twenty four  hundred dollars  um  and uh i suspect that's partially  because it has eight channels um it uh  got that sort of traditional look uh  obviously these things will be rack  mounted and not uh  you know put on a desk but but you can  and you can see my sample over here  and  the back connections are unusual uh for  our hi-fi industry but quite common in  custom install industry in that there  are all these phoenix blocks  um you can see an example in here so i  had to make my own connectors and xlr  connectors out of these guys not  difficult you just have to make sure you  get the pin one two and three correct uh  on this thing  um  uh the app is quite nice and functional  uh i should tell you that these controls  look analog in nature but they don't  they're not analogy i can actually turn  them for a while nothing changes and all  sudden clicks one db and unfortunately  you can't adjust  better than one db  so  as you can see i have a bit of channel  mismatch when i measured it and i could  not compensate for this either would go  too low or too high  the gain is a little bit on the low side  but pretty manageable 25 db i like to  see 29 db but when i set it to max it  stopped at 25 db so this amplifier is  getting a little bit of an advantage  compared to other amps that i test at 29  db  excuse me  the  biggest issue with this amplifier as  you'll see throughout the measurements  is this high level of distortion you can  see that you know that this spike is  around minus 58 db relative to our uh  main tone it's the third harmonic that's  that's quite loud and that sets our  synad which is the sum of noise and  distortion relative to our signal this  is quite uh poor if you look at the uh  uh all the amplifiers that i've tested  which i think is like 170 amplifiers  in last three years that it lands  squarely in the red section which is you  know quite poor uh you know anything  green is is fine and good and then blue  is exceptional so  we really have quite ways to climb in  here  i expect the noise performance to be  poor but this actually is not which is a  good thing some pro amps can be noisy  so at just 5 watts it has 15 bits of  dynamic range this beats a lot of  consumer amps  my target is 96 db but that requires  being in this blue category to usually  hit it and then when you turn it up at  the full volume the noise doesn't get  elevated as as much so it gives you 18  bits of dynamic range so it's high res  ready  i didn't quite like the frequency  response measurements on one hand and  like on another hand in that you can see  instead of being flat in 20 to 20  kilohertz which is what these two  cursors are uh it has uh somewhat  pronounced uh roll off about one and a  half db at 20 kilohertz and the roll off  doesn't stop till about five kilohertz  and then same with bass uh it rolls off  excuse me  down to you know two three db at 10  hertz so as a subwoofer amp you'll have  to compensate for that if you're going  to use it for that  the good news is that even though they  say it's class d when i um switch the  loads from forum to eight home the  frequency response did not change uh  many  less advanced class ds will then show an  overshoot uh or change in response when  i switch from forum to head home there's  no dependency in here so i don't know  that it's class d uh  but maybe it is i suspect it's maybe  more of a class g with a switching  amplifier  but i'll touch on that a little bit  later  crosstalk is good there are consumer  apps that don't do as well  multi-tone shows the problem again with  distortion and now we're you know if we  measure that in in bits equivalent we  get only 13 bits everything below 13  bits gets hammered and  lost in this intermodulation jungle  this is a very disappointing graph in  that we see that the  noise dominates here but unlike these  two other reference amps  distortion starts to dominate before you  even get to half a watt uh and it just  climbs and that's why five watts we had  poor performance and it gets a little  bit better but then it  skyrockets it tells me that it has a  very low level of feedback uh  and that gives us sort of a i don't know  if tube is the right board but it's the  same in some of the poorly designed  consumer amps that don't have as much  feedback so  um anyway so the distortion keeps  climbing  at some point it really clips and that's  about 312 watts but that's very high  distortion  it's so high that when i measure at just  one percent distortion we actually get  less power usually this is the other way  around  so spec is 250 we're getting 220 good  enough um it has no headroom because of  the switching power supplies are  regulated and so they're  you know peak power now and the  continuous power usually the same so  more or less we're at uh 240 watts  go to uh  excuse me at uh 8 ohm uh same problem  happens just distortion climbs  continuously and goes up so not very  good  uh a lot of those tests were one  kilohertz so let's sweep it at uh from  15 kilos down to 20 hertz and we see  this this interesting hump that happens  at all frequencies and gets worse at  higher frequencies this tends to tell me  there's some kind of rail switching  going on like class g class g runs the  amplifier at lower voltage when it  doesn't need as much power and then  switches to a higher voltage and  continues to provide more power and that  gives it efficiency and makes the heat  sink smaller  and  we see that hump in here or it could  also be you know class d designs have  all kinds of weirdness can go on with  them as far as their transfer functions  so  uh not great the whole graph is way up  here and then we have this hump in here  around four or five or ten watts which  is could be the average listening level  you have so you get more distortion at  five watts than you do at almost 200  watts  so  not good uh on this front so  uh instead of laboring the point you  know we can see that this this amplifier  is essentially a distortion factory for  its intended application of driving  uh background sound in a venue and what  have you nobody's going to care but for  hi-fi use i don't think it's that great  of a choice it's expensive at twenty  four hundred dollars  so for home theater could you use it but  yeah it's got copious amount of  distortion i've tested other pro amps  that have much better performance than  this um  anyway you have the data if you want to  buy it anyway uh i'm told the owner  bought it on ebay and then had this  thing serviced before sending it to me  so  now you have the full story okay i'll  make this short and sweet see you in a  future video bye bye

